# VS Code 1.140.0 extension API declaration discovery

This finite source study inventories the official `src/vscode-dts/vscode.d.ts` at commit `07f806f999227108933c2e30515b26eecc1fda74`. The same commit's official package metadata reports version **1.140.0** and MIT licensing. The exact source is 742,274 bytes, SHA256 `a4321528e86dcdcb8450287f2a263055c46a622119cdb186de85ac3b19ce9608`. The selected declaration in the previously authenticated Microsoft portable release is 763,513 bytes, SHA256 `b702638b10d57bb5c61dbe79cb3c071a0bee6a8b8b074de0f8a03f333b4b9270`: its CRLF bytes differ, while replacing CRLF with LF produces exact text equality. No entire carrier/tree authentication or original program execution was repeated here.

The inventory is generated by TypeScript **5.9.3**, obtained from its official npm package metadata and authenticated before execution by the declared SHA512 integrity and SHA1 digest. The selected parser runtime/package/license/notice hashes are in `acquisition.json`; the package archive and JavaScript runtime remain local under `.local/`. No install script, VS Code binary, account, credential or user data is used. `LICENSE-PROVENANCE.md` explains redistribution of the declaration and derived records.

`results/api-inventory.json` has **2,909 public syntactic declaration records**, including **2,850 with directly attached JSDoc** and 59 without it. These records are not atomic features. Their categories include 16 namespaces, 122 classes, 303 interfaces, 64 enums, 154 functions, 394 methods, 1,334 properties, 98 variables, 113 public constructors and 234 enum members. Twenty type aliases, 27 inline function signatures, one call signature, 28 index signatures and the external module complete the declaration count. The 182 inline signature/type-literal-member records are a subset of the 2,909, not extra APIs. Parameters (1,287), type parameters (65), private/protected exclusions (10), and anonymous structural types (91) are separate denominators.

Direct `Event<T>`/`vscode.Event<T>` annotations identify **109 event-typed declarations**, a subset of properties/variables; aliases are not inferred. There are **42 syntactic overload groups** containing 102 declarations (60 beyond one declaration per group). Overloads remain individual source signatures and are not implementation comparisons or additional completed features. Stable IDs bind the qualified name, declaration kind and exact pinned-source UTF16 start; IDs are source-version-specific and do not replace existing capability IDs.

Declaration, parameter, type-parameter, type-expression, heritage and JSDoc spans retain exact UTF16 offsets, UTF8 byte offsets, one-based line/column positions and slice hashes. Original text is obtained from the exact retained source; no normalized text is substituted in these locations. JSDoc tags and 1,496 direct link references retain locations; textual link resolution is a finite qualified-name lookup, not TypeScript semantic binding. `results/source-comment-census.json` independently scans all 2,870 source comments (2,866 JSDoc-shaped), authenticates every one of the 2,851 public JSDoc attachments, and retains the 19 remaining source comments separately. It does not turn all comments into API documentation or synthesize inherited prose.

Of **22 direct heritage references**, 14 link to declarations in this same source and eight remain explicitly external: `Error`, `Iterable` and `PromiseLike` bases. No inherited members are copied into the declaration count. There is no recursive inheritance expansion, generic substitution, override/conflict analysis, semantic typechecking or cross-file/import binding. Mapped/conditional/union/indexed syntax is retained, not expanded into hypothetical members. Zero unsupported declaration forms were encountered in this file; synthetic import/re-export cases are explicitly marked unsupported. This does not mean every TypeScript construct is supported by the collector.

The final source-bound campaign passed **8/8 tests with zero skips**, including qualified scopes, overloads, constructors, events, Unicode/CRLF ranges, private/protected exclusions, inline structures, unresolved external inheritance/imports, malformed syntax, bounds and exact full-file replay. The final build and test processes made ten AST parse calls, and both ended normally with 19 input fingerprints unchanged. The separate comment census made one scanner call, zero AST parses, and retained before/after source fingerprints. Corpus traversal visited 19,034 AST nodes, maximum depth 12. Source input is limited to 2 MiB, AST traversal to 150,000 nodes/depth 256 and 20,000 declaration records; generated inventory is capped at 16 MiB. These are finite collector bounds, not hard real-time preemption or security guarantees for the TypeScript parser.

Preserved attempts include two acquisition assumption failures (notice filename; mistyped vendor archive hash), a missing runner-parent directory, an initial 5/7 test run, and a corrected run stopped at the 4 MiB output cap. Initial fixture comments were on a same-line form TypeScript did not attach as JSDoc; adding line breaks corrected only those fixture inputs. The constructor-kind counter did have an implementation error: a plain-object inherited `constructor` value was combined with numeric counts. It now uses a null-prototype counter, with an explicit regression. The second run's comparison mistakenly tested internal object prototypes against deserialized JSON, causing a giant assertion report; final comparison checks exact serialized data. Raw captured stdout/stderr, source-before/source-after hashes and experiment sources remain intact. Acquisition/directory failures are honestly labeled tool-output transcriptions, not reconstructed raw child streams. The failed counter output remains historical rather than silently replaced.

Acquisition performed eighteen direct HTTP requests across three attempts, plus three separate browser-search opens for initial official source inspection. Three build/test campaigns loaded the parser in six processes; the comment census loaded it once more. The capped campaign has no final parser marker, so its exact last parse count is not claimed. No original VS Code program, workbench, extension host, UI, build or runtime behavior was executed. No production or registry file was changed; original 159 source fingerprints and 232 capability IDs remain separately preserved.

This is a reproducible declaration inventory for one file. It is not every documented workbench feature, settings/commands/keybindings/UI flow, proposed API file, built-in extension contract, or a complete atomic requirement extraction. There is no implemented parity, security parity, performance or superiority claim.

To reproduce with authenticated local prerequisites:

```text
node work/vscode-extension-api-discovery-next/acquire.mjs
node work/vscode-extension-api-discovery-next/run.mjs NEW_UNIQUE_RUN_NAME
node work/vscode-extension-api-discovery-next/comment-census.mjs
```

Use a new isolated copy for reproduction: the final frozen study and its historical attempts should remain unchanged. Fetches are restricted by the collector to the named official source/package URLs and finite response caps; acquisition is not a bundled original-product execution.
